WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:02.860
Last time, weathering broke the rock down.

00:02.860 --> 00:05.264
But broken rock does not stay where it is.

00:05.264 --> 00:08.621
Look at any river in Chennai after heavy monsoon rain.

00:08.621 --> 00:10.080
The water is brown.

00:10.080 --> 00:13.364
That brown color is soil and rock on the move.

00:13.364 --> 00:14.994
Moving it is called erosion.

00:14.994 --> 00:18.201
By the end of this video you should be able to look at a place

00:18.201 --> 00:21.258
and say what is moving the material, and where it is going.

00:21.258 --> 00:22.703
Erosion has three steps.

00:22.703 --> 00:25.132
First, something picks up loose material.

00:25.132 --> 00:27.965
Second, it carries that material somewhere else.

00:27.965 --> 00:29.757
This step is called transport.

00:29.757 --> 00:31.491
Third, it drops the material.

00:31.491 --> 00:33.341
This step is called deposition.

00:33.341 --> 00:36.521
Loose material that is being moved is called sediment.

00:36.521 --> 00:39.412
The idea that connects all three steps is energy.

00:39.412 --> 00:42.996
When there is a lot of energy, more sediment is picked up and

00:42.996 --> 00:46.465
carried. When the energy runs out, the sediment is dropped.

00:46.465 --> 00:50.049
Keep that rule in mind, because it explains almost everything

00:50.049 --> 00:51.206
else in this video.

00:51.206 --> 00:54.674
The things that do the moving are called agents of erosion.

00:54.674 --> 00:58.432
There are four. Water is the first, and it includes rivers, rain

00:58.432 --> 01:01.149
running over the ground, and waves on a coast.

01:01.149 --> 01:02.306
Wind is the second.

01:02.306 --> 01:04.792
Ice is the third, in the form of glaciers.

01:04.792 --> 01:06.121
Gravity is the fourth.

01:06.121 --> 01:08.839
It pulls loose material directly down a slope.

01:08.839 --> 01:12.423
For any place you study, ask which agent is doing most of the

01:12.423 --> 01:16.123
work. In and around Chennai, the answer is almost always water.

01:16.123 --> 01:18.262
Moving water sorts sediment by size.

01:18.262 --> 01:20.633
Slow water can only carry clay and silt.

01:20.633 --> 01:23.928
These are the tiny particles that make water look muddy.

01:23.928 --> 01:25.605
Faster water can carry sand.

01:25.605 --> 01:29.247
A river in flood can roll gravel, and even boulders, along its

01:29.247 --> 01:32.311
bed. When the water slows down, the reverse happens.

01:32.311 --> 01:36.127
The biggest pieces drop first, and the smallest pieces drop last.

01:36.127 --> 01:39.711
That is why you find boulders in steep mountain streams, sand

01:39.711 --> 01:43.874
on beaches and river bars, and fine mud in quiet lagoons and estuaries.

01:43.874 --> 01:47.458
So the size of the grains in a deposit tells you how fast the

01:47.458 --> 01:49.886
water was moving when it left them there.

01:49.886 --> 01:52.199
Where does all this sediment come from?

01:52.199 --> 01:55.147
Think back to the granite in the weathering video.

01:55.147 --> 01:57.518
The feldspar in granite turns into clay.

01:57.518 --> 02:01.102
The quartz does not change chemically, so its hard grains are

02:01.102 --> 02:02.316
left behind as sand.

02:02.316 --> 02:04.629
And some material dissolves completely.

02:04.629 --> 02:08.387
It is carried away invisibly in the water, and this is where the

02:08.387 --> 02:10.005
salt in the sea comes from.

02:10.005 --> 02:12.491
So a granite hill ends up as three things:

02:12.491 --> 02:14.573
sand, clay, and dissolved material.

02:14.573 --> 02:16.827
Each one travels a different distance.

02:16.827 --> 02:19.024
Sand stops at a beach or a river bar.

02:19.024 --> 02:20.874
Clay travels on to quiet water.

02:20.874 --> 02:23.765
Dissolved material goes all the way to the ocean.

02:23.765 --> 02:27.498
Gravity can move material without help from anything else.

02:27.498 --> 02:31.092
On a slope, gravity pulls loose material downward, and part of

02:31.092 --> 02:33.047
that pull acts along the slope.

02:33.047 --> 02:36.743
Friction, and the strength of the material, hold it in place.

02:36.743 --> 02:39.267
When the pull wins, the material moves.

02:39.267 --> 02:41.012
This is called mass wasting.

02:41.012 --> 02:44.853
It can be very slow, only a few millimeters a year, or it can

02:44.853 --> 02:46.735
be sudden, as in a landslide.

02:46.735 --> 02:48.677
Water is usually the trigger.

02:48.677 --> 02:52.525
Heavy rain adds weight, fills the spaces between the grains, and

02:52.525 --> 02:56.319
weakens the slope. That is why landslides in the hills of South

02:56.319 --> 02:59.885
India, such as the Nilgiris and the Western Ghats, happen mostly

02:59.885 --> 03:00.885
during the monsoon.

03:00.885 --> 03:04.718
The last two agents matter less here, but you should be able to

03:04.718 --> 03:08.491
recognize them. Air is much less dense than water, so wind can

03:08.491 --> 03:10.227
only move dry sand and dust.

03:10.227 --> 03:14.120
It matters in deserts and on dry beaches, where it builds dunes.

03:14.120 --> 03:17.893
On the Chennai coast in the dry season, wind blows sand inland

03:17.893 --> 03:19.929
from the beach. Ice is different.

03:19.929 --> 03:21.725
A glacier has enormous power.

03:21.725 --> 03:25.738
It can carve a whole valley and carry boulders the size of houses.

03:25.738 --> 03:28.373
Glaciers are a major agent in the Himalaya.

03:28.373 --> 03:32.205
There are none anywhere near Chennai, so for a Chennai location

03:32.205 --> 03:33.523
you can rule ice out.

03:33.523 --> 03:36.098
So what does erosion look like in Chennai?

03:36.098 --> 03:37.655
Three things matter most.

03:37.655 --> 03:41.787
Rivers, such as the Cooum and the Adyar, move most of their sediment

03:41.787 --> 03:43.164
during monsoon floods.

03:43.164 --> 03:46.458
Waves move sand along the coast every day of the year.

03:46.458 --> 03:50.530
And on slopes, such as riverbanks and road cuts, material collapses

03:50.530 --> 03:53.105
after heavy rain. Notice one more pattern.

03:53.105 --> 03:54.483
Erosion is not steady.

03:54.483 --> 03:58.555
Most of the work is done in short bursts, during floods and storms.

03:58.555 --> 04:02.448
One cyclone can move more sediment than a whole year of ordinary

04:02.448 --> 04:04.656
days. Check yourself with three questions.

04:04.656 --> 04:06.718
Pause the video if you need to.

04:06.718 --> 04:10.670
One. A river in flood slows down as it reaches flat land.

04:10.670 --> 04:13.928
Which does it drop first, gravel or mud?

04:13.928 --> 04:18.327
Two. After a week of heavy rain, the side of a road cut collapses.

04:18.327 --> 04:20.608
Which agent moved the material?

04:20.608 --> 04:24.189
Three. Dry sand from the beach piles up against a wall.

04:24.189 --> 04:26.572
Which agent? Here are the answers.

04:26.572 --> 04:30.248
The gravel drops first, because the biggest pieces need the most

04:30.248 --> 04:34.243
energy. The road cut is gravity, with water as the trigger.

04:34.243 --> 04:36.913
And the sand against the wall was moved by wind.

04:36.913 --> 04:38.549
Now go on to today's task.
